}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391461 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391461 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391461 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186391463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391461 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aafde8dd-f176-57c0-adc4-2c7ebf5d023e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391462 #edb/ref 1080 "unbounded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391462 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391462 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a9e6f595-8087-5dbd-8139-304da120ec34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391463 #edb/ref 1080 "bounded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391463 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd4b344f-3032-50e7-811d-0221005ce729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391464 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391464 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391464 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391464 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391464 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186391465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391464 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d36b213e-45b1-50bc-990e-06b1564318ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391465 #edb/ref 1080 "an ellipse" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391465 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860454